## Summary

- **Model:** an entropy-weighted TOPSIS multi-criteria decision model, with weights adjusted for patient severity and clinically based time windows.
- **What it does:** ranks the facilities that can give blood and are reachable in time, on drive time, blood capability and the services the patient's situation needs, then returns the top 3 with reasons. Capability data is missing for most candidates, so facilities with confirmed service data are ranked separately from — and always ahead of — facilities with no service data at all; the latter are only used as a fallback, ranked on drive time and blood level alone, never on a guessed services number (see [Unknown vs. confirmed-absent](#criteria)).
- **Why:** there is no record of which facility was the "right" choice for past patients, so no model can learn from examples; and nobody on the team can justify hand-picked weights. Entropy weighting derives the weights from the data; severity adjusts them; TOPSIS turns them into one explainable score.

## Inputs

**From the responder:**

| Input | Values |
|---|---|
| Patient location | Latitude and longitude |
| Scenario | Pregnant or just gave birth, bleeding · Accident or injury, bleeding · Severe weakness or anemia, no injury · Not sure / other |

The responder does not judge severity. Each scenario has a fixed urgency (see [Time windows](#time-windows)); every bleeding case and "not sure" is treated as high urgency, because under-triage costs more than over-triage.

**From the data:**

| Source | Used for | Coverage caveat |
|---|---|---|
| Blood Bank Directory (2,823 licensed blood banks) | Location, blood components available, 24x7 service | Components/24x7 are blank for a meaningful share of rows — blank means unrecorded, not absent (see [Unknown vs. confirmed-absent](#criteria)) |
| VF facilities (10,000, cleaned and verified) | Location, facilities with a blood bank, services (C-section, surgery, trauma care, ICU, and so on) | Only ~475 of the ~3,270 candidates in the final pool are VF-linked with service rows; the rest have no service data to draw on at all |

Not "assumed cleaned and correct" — verified against the actual `blood_points_v2.csv` /
`facility_services_v2.csv` files, both known to be extracted from web text with real coverage
gaps, per the project's own data caveats.

## How the model works

1. **Set urgency and time window** from the scenario: high urgency 60 minutes, lower urgency 120 minutes.
2. **Build the candidate pool:** every place that can give blood (directory blood banks plus VF facilities with a blood bank, merged where they are the same place), with an estimated drive time from the patient.
3. **Keep candidates within the time window.**
   - **None:** show the 3 closest, with a warning that none is reachable in time.
   - **One:** recommend it.
   - **Several:** split into two tiers before scoring anything — see step 4.
4. **Split into a confirmed tier and an unconfirmed tier.** Confirmed = the facility has actual service data (`needed_services_confirmed`). Unconfirmed = no service record exists for it at all (true for most directory-listed blood banks).
   - **At least one confirmed candidate reachable:** rank *only the confirmed tier*, on all three criteria (drive time, blood level, needed services) — steps 5 to 7 below.
   - **No confirmed candidate reachable** (every reachable facility is unconfirmed): rank the unconfirmed tier instead, on **drive time and blood level only** — `needed_services` is left out of the math entirely rather than guessed at. Every result from this tier is flagged "capability not confirmed — call ahead."
5. **Entropy weights:** each criterion's weight comes from how much it varies among this tier's candidates. A criterion that is the same for every candidate cannot separate them and gets almost no weight; one that varies a lot gets more.
6. **Severity boost:** multiply the weight of the criterion that matters most at this severity (high urgency: drive time × 2; lower urgency: needed services × 2 — only applies when ranking the confirmed tier, since the unconfirmed tier doesn't use that criterion at all), then rescale the weights to sum to 1.
7. **TOPSIS score:** each candidate's closeness to an ideal facility and distance from the worst, as a score from 0 to 1 (over whichever criteria that tier uses).
8. **Output:** the top 3, each with drive time, score, the weights used, and any gaps (for example "no C-section found here; may need transfer") — plus the unconfirmed-tier flag whenever the recommendation came from step 4's fallback.

This reproduces the behaviour the team wanted without hand-set weights:

- **Rural:** few options, with very different drive times. Drive time varies most, so it gets the most weight and the nearest reachable option wins.
- **City:** many options at similar drive times. Drive time barely varies, so blood level and services decide.

